{
  "concept": "Chaining resolves hash collisions by storing every key that hashes to the same slot in one linked list, so the hash function h alone decides how the actual keys K are partitioned across the slots of T and how long each chain becomes.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Outer dark-gray ellipse labeled 'U (universe of keys)'",
    "Inner light-gray ellipse labeled 'K (actual keys)' nested inside U",
    "Eight filled black dots inside K labeled k1…k8, each annotated with its numeric key value (k1=10, k2=14, k3=7, k4=19, k5=5, k6=17, k7=23, k8=8)",
    "Vertical column of slots representing the hash table, labeled 'T' above it",
    "Slot index labels 0…m-1 alongside the column",
    "Empty slots drawn as dark-gray rectangles with a diagonal slash (nil)",
    "Non-empty slots drawn as white/light rectangles containing a short horizontal pointer stub",
    "Curved/straight arrows from each key dot in K crossing to the slot h(k) it maps to",
    "Arrows from each non-empty slot rightward to the head of its chain",
    "Doubly linked list chains to the right of T; each node is a three-cell box: left-pointer cell, key-label cell, right-pointer cell",
    "Forward and backward arrows between consecutive chain nodes; diagonal slash in end cells for nil prev/next",
    "Default chains reproducing the textbook figure: slot with [k1,k4], slot with [k5,k2,k7], slot with [k3], slot with [k8,k6]",
    "Chain-length badge next to each non-empty slot",
    "Readout of load factor alpha = n/m and longest-chain length",
    "Legend: slash = nil pointer, gray slot = empty, white slot = has chain"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "hashFunction",
      "type": "code_editor",
      "teaches": "That h(k) is the only thing that determines which slot a key lands in — and therefore that collision frequency and chain lengths are a property of the hash function, not of the data structure",
      "label": "Define the hash function h(k)",
      "entry_point": "h(k)",
      "api": [
        { "op": "m()", "does": "number of slots in T (set by the table size slider)" },
        { "op": "keyValue()", "does": "the numeric value of the key currently being hashed (same as the argument k)" },
        { "op": "chainLength(j)", "does": "how many keys are already chained in slot j — read-only, for experimenting with probing-free diagnostics" },
        { "op": "note(text)", "does": "attach your own comment to this insertion frame; shown in the replay caption" },
        { "op": "mark(j)", "does": "highlight slot j in the current frame (annotation only, does not move keys)" },
        { "op": "returnSlot(j)", "does": "the value you return from h(k); the driver draws the arrow to slot j and prepends the key's node to that chain — the ONLY way a key enters the table" }
      ],
      "frame": "one key's insertion: the whole K circle with the current key dot highlighted, the arrow drawn from it to slot h(k), the slot recoloured if it was nil, and the new three-cell node prepended to that slot's chain",
      "input": { "label": "Key values k1…k8", "default": "10, 14, 7, 19, 5, 17, 23, 8" },
      "sample_code": "function h(k) {\n  // classic division method: spread keys over all m slots\n  note('h(' + k + ') = ' + (k % m()));\n  return k % m();\n}",
      "buggy_code": "function h(k) {\n  // BUG: dividing by a small constant ignores m, so every key\n  // crowds into the first few slots and one chain grows to length n\n  return Math.floor(k / 10);\n}",
      "success_check": "every key ends up in exactly one chain, no returned slot is outside 0…m-1, and the union of all chains equals K"
    },
    {
      "id": "tableSize",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "How the number of slots m interacts with h to set the load factor alpha = n/m and the expected chain length",
      "label": "Table size m",
      "range": [3, 15, 1],
      "default": 9
    },
    {
      "id": "inspectSlot",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "That a search costs one hash computation plus a walk of exactly one chain, so cost is proportional to that chain's length"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The textbook mapping",
      "narration": "With h(k) = k mod 9, the eight actual keys fall into four slots: k1,k4 share one, k5,k2,k7 share another, k3 sits alone, and k8,k6 pair up. Every other slot stays nil, marked by a slash.",
      "state": { "tableSize": 9 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Watch the collisions happen",
      "narration": "Step the replay one key at a time: each frame draws one arrow from K into T and prepends one node. A collision is simply a second arrow arriving at a slot that is already white.",
      "state": { "tableSize": 9 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Click a slot to search it",
      "narration": "Clicking a non-empty slot highlights its whole chain — the exact list a search for one of those keys must traverse. Slot with three keys costs three node visits; the singleton costs one.",
      "state": { "tableSize": 9 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Shrink the table",
      "narration": "Drop m to 3 and the same hash function crams all eight keys into three slots: alpha jumps above 2.5 and chains balloon. The data never changed — only h and m did.",
      "state": { "tableSize": 3 }
    },
    {
      "title": "A poor hash, same table",
      "narration": "Now paste the buggy h(k) = floor(k/10) back at m = 9: nearly every key returns slot 0 or 1, so one chain holds almost all of K and lookups degrade toward linear search.",
      "state": { "tableSize": 9 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Spread them out again",
      "narration": "Return to h(k) = k mod m with m = 13 (a prime): chains flatten to length one or two and most searches touch a single node.",
      "state": { "tableSize": 13 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Chaining never fails — collisions just make one list longer — so the whole performance of a hash table lives in the single line of code that computes h(k).",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Three-band left-to-right layout. Left third: two nested ellipses, outer dark gray labeled 'U (universe of keys)', inner light gray labeled 'K (actual keys)'; eight small filled black dots scattered in the inner ellipse labeled k1…k8 in italic subscript style, with their numeric values in small gray text beside each. Middle: a tall narrow vertical stack of equal rectangles labeled 'T' at the top, index numbers to its left; empty slots filled mid-gray with a thin diagonal slash, occupied slots white with a short horizontal pointer stub and a small chain-length badge. Thin black arrows with open arrowheads run from key dots across the gap to their slot; arrows may cross each other, which is part of the lesson. Right two-fifths: horizontal doubly linked chains, each node a light-gray three-cell box (prev | key | next), forward arrow from a next-cell to the following node and a backward arrow returning, terminal cells slashed for nil. Colors stay grayscale like the textbook except that the key currently being inserted, its arrow, and its new node glow in a single accent color (e.g. orange), and a clicked slot's entire chain highlights in a second accent (e.g. blue). Small readout panel near T showing m, n = 8, alpha = n/m, and longest chain. Layout must reflow gracefully when m changes and when chains grow long."
}

